**Read:** [Leak calibration — Ehrlich and Tison, NIST SP 250-38](../sources/Leak-Calibration-Ehrlich-Tison.lingua#r_622GG9PXEYJ8FGEPWD4P1DMTT7), leak-comparator, temperature-dependence and quality-control sections.

A reference leak is a physical gas-flow artifact, not an immutable number. Its flow can depend on temperature, reservoir state, gas and elapsed time. An orifice and a permeation element follow different mechanisms and need different models.

For an explicitly assumed activated molar-permeation model ṅ(T)=A exp(−B/T), throughput expressed at that same gas temperature is L(T)=RTṅ(T). Thus L(T)/L(T₀)=(T/T₀)exp[B(1/T₀−1/T)] and its logarithmic temperature sensitivity is 1/T+B/T². Molar flow, or throughput referred to one fixed reference temperature, instead has relative sensitivity B/T². Keep the temperature dependence of the artifact distinct from the temperature used to express its throughput. The model is conditional on the element and range, not universal for every leak.

With hypothetical B=3000 K and T₀=300 K, the local relative sensitivity of L(T) is approximately 0.036667 K⁻¹; that of molar flow is 0.033333 K⁻¹. A temperature indication on an outer casing may differ from the active element, especially during a transient. Reservoir depletion and contamination can add further drift.

**Work:** Compare a local linear correction with the full model over a stated range. Propagate temperature and coefficient uncertainty. Explain why a calibration at one temperature does not establish flow at another without a validated dependence and adequate thermal equilibration.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
